      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The objective of this study was to describe the prevalence and correlates of regular napping among older adults."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The National Sleep Foundation's \"2003 Sleep in America Poll,\" a 20-minute telephone interview that focused on the topic of \"sleep and aging\" (N = 1,506 adults 55-84 years of age)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Overall, 15% of respondents reported regular napping, ranging in prevalence from 10% among those 55-64 years of age to 25% among those 75-84 years of age. In addition to older age and a strong association with excessive daytime sleepiness, other factors that independently increased prevalence included a diagnosis of depression, bodily pain, and nocturia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Regular napping is common among older adults. Longitudinal studies of napping behavior and health status are needed to establish risk factors other than excessive daytime sleepiness."
        }
      ]},
